DESCRIPTION:Launch your systematic review with free summer series! \n\n \n\
 nInterested in conducting a systematic review? The Health Sciences Library
 ’s Systematic Review Summer Workshop Series will get you started in eigh
 t bite-sized sessions. Sign up for all of them\, or just the ones you need
 . \n\n \n\nThis free virtual series will provide researchers with tools an
 d resources to confidently navigate the systematic review process.  \n\n \
 n\nWho should attend? \n\n \n\nThis series is intended for individuals and
  research teams who are planning to conduct a systematic literature review
  or want more information about systematic reviews. It is also relevant fo
 r those conducting scoping reviews or other similar evidence synthesis pro
 jects. No prior knowledge or experience with systematic reviews is require
 d. The instruction is aimed toward health reviews\, but open to anyone at 
 UNC-Chapel Hill or the UNC Medical Center.   \n\n \n\nWhat will be covered
 ? \n\n \n\nWe’ve broken down the systematic review process from ideation
  through publication into eight one-hour lessons. Each lunchtime session r
 uns on Wednesdays from noon to 1 p.m. \n\n \n\nJune 10 - Introduction to S
 ystematic Review Methods \n\nJune 17 - What Type of Review Should You Choo
 se? \n\nJune 24 - Project Management and Helpful Tools for Conducting Syst
 ematic Reviews \n\nJuly 8 - Literature Searching for Systematic Reviews \n
 \nJuly 15 - Introduction to Covidence \n\nJuly 22 - Managing Data\, Writin
 g and Publishing your Review \n\nJuly 29 - Predictive Artificial Intellige
 nce and Automation Tools
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Literature Searching for Systematic Reviews (Class 4 of 8 in the Sy
 stematic Review Summer Workshop Series)
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/literature-searching-for-syste
 matic-reviews-class-4-of-8-in-the-systematic-review-summer-workshop-series
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260731T040456Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53083063187528
DTSTART:20260708T160000Z
DTEND:20260708T170000Z
DESCRIPTION:Anxiety is a highly uncomfortable feeling\, a lingering presenc
 e that causes physical and mental tension\, fills the mind with endless 
 “what ifs\,” and is capable of spiralling into episodes of paralyzing 
 anxiety attacks. People with anxiety will typically focus on reducing or e
 liminating it as the main problem\, whereas the real problem may lie elsew
 here\, with psychological avoidance. Psychological avoidance is a quick fi
 x to anxiety. It offers immediate emotional relief\, but it’s fleeting a
 nd comes at a heavy cost. When we avoid certain situations\, decisions\, o
 r actions\, it can ultimately lead to even greater anxiety and additional 
 problems.\nFor this topic\, we’ll examine the role of anxiety\, our mist
 aken belief that we should always be “feeling good\,” and the psycholo
 gical effects of avoidance. We’ll explore specific cognitive-behavioral 
 strategies for managing avoidance and dealing with anxiety. This is a comp
 anion course to the topic\, “Managing Worry and Anxiety.”\n\nClick on 
 Carolina Talent registration link and select the “Request” button to a
 utomatically enroll.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Psychological Avoidance & Its Impact on Your Mental Health
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/psychological-avoidance-its-im
 pact-on-your-mental-health
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260731T040456Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52958661069171
DTSTART:20260708T170000Z
DTEND:20260708T180000Z
DESCRIPTION:Want to do more with AI? Agents can help. AI agents are designe
 d to work toward specific goals and complete tasks with less step-by-step 
 direction from the user. While they can be powerful and efficient tools\, 
 giving them too much autonomy can also create problems. In this workshop\,
  you’ll learn what AI agents can do\, where their limits are\, and how t
 o create and use your own using the UNC Library’s PromptLab. No prior ex
 perience with agents is required\, though participants who are new to gene
 rative AI may find it helpful to review our brief training videos before t
 aking this workshop.\n\n \n\nThis workshop is offered by the University Li
 braries and will be led by librarians Michele Hayslett and Lorin Bruckner.
 \n\n \n\nThis workshop is being offered remotely via Zoom only. Registrant
 s will receive a Zoom link\; those on the waitlist will not receive a Zoom
  link unless they are bumped up to a registered spot.\n\n \n\nAll registra
 nts however\, will receive access to the meeting recording and resources.\
 n\n \n\nYou can register through the Library Calendar.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Agentic AI
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/agentic-ai
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260731T040456Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53372195530132
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260710
DESCRIPTION:Care and Concern: Featuring works of modern and contemporary ar
 t acquired by the Ackland since 2020\, Care and Concern explores how artis
 ts communicate their cares and concerns about the world around them. Spann
 ing media\, including painting\, sculpture\, and works on paper\, the sele
 ction centers on both the personal feelings expressed by artists and the e
 motions they hope to inspire in viewers. The works of art range from expre
 ssions of love and disappointment to anxiety and solace. These sentiments 
 shape how individuals navigate interpersonal relationships and societal sy
 stems through attention\, empathy\, and critique.\n\n \n\nThis exhibition 
 is made possible by support from Laura and Walter Elcock.\n\n \n\nContinen
 tal Shift: This exhibition will present selected works of African art adde
 d to the collection over the past five years\, with a special emphasis on 
 metalwork\, textiles\, beadwork\, and ceramics\, from across the African c
 ontinent\, including eastern and southern regions. This focus reflects the
  Museum’s recent efforts to pay special attention to materials and geogr
 aphic areas that complement our traditional emphasis on figurative woodcar
 ving from West and Central Africa.\n\n \n\nContinental Shift is part of a 
 long-running series of shows focused on newly acquired works across the Ac
 kland’s collecting areas\, reflecting our dynamic and expansive project 
 of collection-building.\n\n \n\nThis exhibition is made possible by suppor
 t from Laura and Walter Elcock.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Ackland Exhibitions: “Care and Concern” and “Continental Shif
 t”
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/care-and-concern-recent-acquis
 itions-of-modern-and-contemporary-art
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260731T040456Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53083090185392
DTSTART:20260710T160000Z
