X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260731T040457Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53379073586000
DTSTART:20260914T233000Z
DTEND:20260915T010000Z
DESCRIPTION:Yves Montand\, Jean-Louis Trintignant\, and Irène Papas headli
 ne this electrifying political thriller. A politician is assassinated in b
 road daylight\, setting in motion an investigation that gradually exposes 
 the distance between official narratives and lived reality. Racing ahead w
 ith remarkable urgency\, Z channels late-1960s anti-authoritarian currents
  and the growing sense that democratic institutions can no longer be taken
  at face value. \nThis film will be introduced by Professor Henry Veggian\
 , UNC-Chapel Hill Department of English and Comparative Literature. \nIt w
 ill be screened at 7:30 p.m. at The Lumina Theater (620 Market St\, Chapel
  Hill\, NC 27516). Tickets are free for all audience members\, and registr
 ation is required via The Lumina’s webpage. When this link is live\, cli
 ck to reserve your free ticket. \nUNC-Chapel Hill students can receive CLE
  credit for attending by scanning the QR code at the event. \n  \nAbout th
 e Series: \nJoin the Ackland Film Forum this fall for Crime Thrillers from
  the Americas: Snapshots of Social Anxiety\, a series that accompanies the
  Ackland exhibition Care and Concern: Recent Acquisitions of Modern and Co
 ntemporary Art. \nAs vehicles of social commentary and stylistic bravura\,
  crime thrillers often register the pressures and contradictions of their 
 historical moment. Across nearly half a century\, the films in this series
  stage encounters with political power\, public trust\, inequality\, and a
 mbition through assassination plots\, botched robberies\, and media-driven
  spectacle. \nFrom the crises of the late 1960s to the intensified media e
 nvironments of the twenty-first century\, these works show how genre cinem
 a can capture social anxiety in real time. Each film stands both as a grip
 ping narrative and as a record of a world under strain\, where institution
 s falter and everyday life becomes fraught with conflict. \nThe Ackland Fi
 lm Forum is co-organized by the Ackland Art Museum and UNC Film Studies\, 
 housed in the UNC-Chapel Hill Department of English and Comparative Litera
 ture.
LOCATION:The Lumina Theater\, 620 Market Street\, Chapel Hill\, 27516\, Uni
 ted States
SUMMARY:Ackland Film Forum: "Z" (Costa-Gavras\, 1969)
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/ackland-film-forum-z-costa-gav
 ras-1969
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
