DESCRIPTION:This interactive online workshop will provide an introduction t
 o focus groups\, a data collection method used in qualitative research. To
 pics covered will include focus group methodology\, considerations for pla
 nning a focus group\, development of focus group discussion guides\, focus
  group facilitation skills\, and tips for conducting virtual focus groups.
  Attendees will also observe or participate in a mini "mock" virtual focus
  group session.\n\n \n\nThis event is part of the NC TraCS Qualitative Res
 earch Training Series and may be recorded. Materials such as slides or han
 douts will be shared with documented attendees only.\n\n \n\nPresenters:\n
 MaryBeth Grewe\, MPH\nProgram Manager\, Qualitative Research Service\nRese
 arch Specialist\, Patient and Community Engagement in Research (PaCER) Pro
 gram\n\n \n\nSimone Frank\, MPH\nSenior Project Manager\, Patient and Comm
 unity Engagement in Research (PaCER) Program\nResearch Specialist\, Qualit
 ative Research Service\n\n \n\nRegister\n\n \n\nPlease contact MaryBeth Gr
 ewe at marybeth_grewe@med.unc.edu if you have questions about this trainin
 g.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Introduction to Focus Groups
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/introduction-to-focus-groups-8
 664
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260731T040457Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53055342978173
DTSTART:20260924T120000Z
DTEND:20260924T210000Z
DESCRIPTION:Art and Art History\n\nUnder the Tent: Whirling & Flickering\n\
 nAugust 20-October 15\, 2026\n\nReception: August 20\, 5-7 pm\n\n \n\nDraw
 n together in graduate school by their mutual love of experimentation and 
 play\, Michelle Inez Hinojosa and Barbara Pearsall have built a collaborat
 ive practice around analogue animation and vibrant abstraction. Together\,
  their work explores optical toys\, immersive installations\, and movement
 . Under the Tent: Whirling & Flickering features kaleidoscopic imagery\, f
 lickering abstractions\, projection mapping\, a hidden trapdoor\, and hand
 -cranked wheels that transform what viewers see. After searching for the p
 erfect venue\, they are thrilled to bring their collaborative installation
  to UNC. Their first public artwork\, Turnabout Folly\, will debut in Lans
 ing\, Michigan in Fall 2026.\n\n \n\nMichelle Hinojosa works at the inters
 ection of painting\, poetry\, and fiber art\, where she considers memory\,
  intergenerational trauma\, and migration. Born in central Texas\, by way 
 of the fields of California and Northern Mexico\, Michelle uplifts traditi
 ons of making that have provided much-needed stability to a family displac
 ed. Michelle Hinojosa is an Assistant Professor of Art at Pennsylvania Sta
 te University.\n\n \n\nBarbara Pearsall is an artist and educator based in
  Michigan. Her art practice is attentive to how deviation from material co
 nventions can subvert or metamorphose the role of institutions\, where her
  philosophical and political concerns manifest through material exploratio
 ns of the built environment. Her pieces seek to create illusory and unusua
 l architectural conditions as a form of resistance\, interrupting real-wor
 ld functionality to allow for alternative systems of order. She is an Assi
 stant Professor of Foundations & Sculpture at Michigan State University.\n
 \n \n\nAdmission: Free\n\nGallery Hours: Monday-Friday\, 8 am-5 pm\n\n \n\
 nA weeknight or daytime permit is now required after 5:00 pm on weekdays. 
 No permit is required from 5:00 pm Friday through 7:30 am Monday. A $1.00 
 one-night pass is available in selected lots. More information can be foun
 d HERE.\n\n \n\nFor more information\, please contact: Annette Lawrence\, 
 annettel@unc.edu
GEO:35.912392;-79.054366
LOCATION:Hanes Art Center\, John and June Allcott Gallery
SUMMARY:John and June Allcott Gallery: Michelle Inez Hinojosa and Barbara P
 earsall
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/john-and-june-allcott-gallery-
 michelle-inez-hinojosa-and-barbara-pearsall
CATEGORIES:Exhibits
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260731T040457Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53194956217002
DTSTART:20260924T140000Z
DTEND:20260924T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:This course explores the potential applications of generative A
 I (gen AI) for survey research. Designed for survey practitioners\, data s
 cientists\, and social scientists\, it provides an introduction to Gen AI 
 – especially large language models (LLMs) – and delves into their appl
 ications across the survey lifecycle. Participants will gain foundational 
 knowledge of how AI technologies like LLMs work\, understand its implicati
 ons for survey data quality (in relation to frameworks such as Total Surve
 y Error)\, and learn practical strategies for integrating GenAI into surve
 y design\, data collection\, and analysis. Rather than focusing on the tec
 hnical aspects of LLMs\, this course will focus on applied AI research\, d
 rawing on empirical evidence of how LLMs perform in areas such as conversa
 tional interviewing and synthetic response generation. We will pay particu
 lar attention to model evaluation\, responsible management of risks\, user
  experience design (UXD)\, and the augmentation (not replacement) of human
  expertise.\n\nRegistration and fee are required for Zoom link access.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Generative AI and Survey Research: Foundations\, Applications\, and
  the Respondent Experience
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/generative-ai-and-survey-resea
 rch-foundations-applications-and-the-respondent-experience-1406
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260731T040457Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52259562235016
DTSTART:20260924T170000Z
DTEND:20260924T180000Z
DESCRIPTION:AI in Research: Driving Innovation Forward\n\nDiscover how arti
 ficial intelligence can streamline workflows\, uncover hidden patterns\, a
 nd empower breakthroughs in your field. Learn practical applications of AI
  through Odum Institute AIR series that can revolutionize your research pr
 ocess.\n\nAI\, for example ChatGPT\, may be used to construct code for sof
 tware packages and programming languages commonly used by social scientist
 s. Examples include SAS\, Stata and R. The quality of responses has varied
  to this point and may continue to at the time of the class. Examples will
  be given.\n\nYou must have a UNC Chapel Hill Onyen to register and attend
  this course. No registration fees required but you must register to secur
 e your spot and receive the Zoom link.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:AI in Research Series – AI for Programming
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/ai-in-research-series-ai-for-p
 rogramming-9388
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
